When n = 2, local extrema of f(x, y) are precisely the pits and peaks of the surface given by the graph of z = f(x, y), as suggested by Figure 4.12. 
We emphasize our use of the adjective "local". 
When a local maximum of a function f occurs at a point a, this means that the values of f at points near a can be no larger, not that all values of f are no larger. 
Indeed, f may have local maxima and no global (or absolute) maximum. 
